# 11. Emerging Policy Problem: Administrative Durability

The wildlife system demonstrates why institutional durability deserves independent attention.

Conservation policy can change through:

* legislation;
* regulation;
* executive action;
* appropriations;
* agency guidance;
* staffing;
* litigation;
* court decisions;
* administrative reorganization.

Some flexibility is necessary.

But excessive instability can undermine long-term conservation because biological systems do not reset when political administrations change.

A species does not become more resilient because a new four-year policy cycle begins.

This suggests that durable conservation institutions should contain:

* clearly defined statutory responsibilities;
* transparent performance measures;
* recurring scientific review;
* publicly accessible data;
* long-term monitoring;
* institutional memory;
* adaptive rather than arbitrary management;
* mechanisms for revising ineffective policies without destabilizing effective ones.

