# 12. Preliminary Gap Analysis

The evidence reviewed so far suggests several areas requiring deeper investigation.

### Gap 1 — Preventive conservation capacity

The United States has mechanisms for proactive conservation, but their scale, funding, and effectiveness require systematic assessment.

### Gap 2 — Cross-jurisdictional species management

Species that cross states, federal lands, Tribal lands, and private property require coordination mechanisms that do not always correspond to political boundaries.

### Gap 3 — Outcome measurement

The conservation system has extensive monitoring capacity, but performance measures are not necessarily standardized across programs.

### Gap 4 — Private-land conservation

Many species cannot be conserved entirely on public lands.

The effectiveness and durability of incentives for private-land conservation therefore require greater attention.

### Gap 5 — Data interoperability

The country possesses extensive wildlife information, but its usefulness depends on whether datasets can be combined and interpreted consistently.

### Gap 6 — Long-term funding

Many ecological processes occur over decades, while funding decisions frequently occur over much shorter periods.

### Gap 7 — Early-warning systems

The project should investigate whether national monitoring can reliably detect population decline early enough to enable comparatively inexpensive intervention.

### Gap 8 — Post-recovery conservation

Delisting or downlisting should not automatically mean that conservation attention disappears.

Post-recovery monitoring and stewardship require explicit consideration.

